#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134767} :activity/id #uuid "912bbbc1-7dd5-5812-ba95-2d17ed90bd7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050270 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135091 :tutorial/content "How do we represent a double integral over a type II region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+2y) \\, \\textrm d A, \n%= \\int_a^b \\int_c^d (x+2y) \\,\\textrm d y\\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$  is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0, y=1$ and the curve $x=e^y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09fa628c26853d48f3775945e856763b5a996c431785ecf4194c879ee881aa78.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is indeed a type II region. \nTo determine the right and left functions, we draw a horizontal arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/47/47fffd047fe12aacfbcc98fa34750e7afcb32dd06618f2553c65432828fff328.png)\n\nHence, the region $D$ can be defined as \n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}0} \\leq y  \\leq {\\color{blue}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{blue}e^y} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{(\\text{lower limit})}^{(\\text{upper limit})}[{\\int }_{(\\text{left function})}^{(\\text{right function})}(x+2y)\\text{ }dx]\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}(x+2y)\\text{ }dx]\\text{ }dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that when we have a type II region, \n\n- the limits for $x$ are functions of $y,$ and\n\n- the limits for $y$ are constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a9a9898-50b3-5263-9da1-1228ed4ee5e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4318 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "212536fd-085f-5bdf-babc-a919d333a568" :step/math-academy-id 26243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133659 :question/id #uuid "792b0c63-15eb-5b0e-b5d6-01ae5b68eeca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050828}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382447}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} :step/id #uuid "cde6f31a-9259-5126-ab62-92a7120fde39" :step/math-academy-id 26244 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133660 :question/id #uuid "e005ebb9-6c14-529e-be8f-9b837e45eef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382475}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "38689b31-f1db-5afc-801c-860cb6fc929b" :step/math-academy-id 26245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135092 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{e^y} (x+2) \\ \\mathrm{d}  x \\, \\mathrm{d}  y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type II region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $x,$ we treat $y$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $y.$ Once again, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $x$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}(x+2)\\text{ }dx]dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x]}_{0}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}+2{e}^{y})\\text{ }dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}+2{e}^{y})\\text{ }dy & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{4}+2{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}+2e-(\\frac{{e}^{0}}{4}+2{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+8e-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf856bdc-a6e0-55d3-a44c-78b2e569f5e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4319 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "6fd8b1b4-a45f-595a-b748-1d95ba70387d" :step/math-academy-id 26246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133661 :question/id #uuid "ebd5bc16-2df2-5014-a1a2-bdebf2afa195" :question/math-academy-id "e-7136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381019}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"} :step/id #uuid "97348c88-b232-5f44-bd21-15af51c5d116" :step/math-academy-id 26247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133662 :question/id #uuid "ad2ef01a-d49d-5b17-9066-d5c3970caf31" :question/math-academy-id "e-7137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382600}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"} :step/id #uuid "0d46d822-c664-59b7-8ccb-0874593592f6" :step/math-academy-id 26248}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134027}]}
